The only obvious security concern is in the `weekly-digest-address` route. The `email` field from a client request is concatenated straight into a shell command:

```js
function buildCommand(email) {
  return 'mail -s "Weekly digest" ' + email + ' < /srv/app/digest.txt';
}
```

This string is then passed to `child_process.exec`, which runs it in a shell. Because the input is never validated or escaped, an attacker can supply malicious shell syntax (e.g., `a@b.com; rm -rf /`) that will be executed. This is classic command‑injection—untrusted input reaches a dangerous sink (`exec`) without sanitization.

All other routes check path names, limit input length, or avoid native code execution, so they are safe. The identified vulnerability, however, renders the application insecure.

VERDICT: VULNERABLE
